---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import Prose from '../../components/Prose.astro';
import { localizedPath } from '../../i18n';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  locale="en"
  title="Pelerin privacy policy — Luvita"
  description="Privacy policy of the Pelerin desktop app: it masks text on your computer and sends Luvita your account, plan and counts, never your text."
  paths={{ tr: 'pelerin-gizlilik', en: 'pelerin-privacy' }}
>
  <Hero eyebrow="Legal" title="Pelerin: privacy policy" />

  <section class="container">
    <Prose>
      <p>Version 0.2 · Last updated: 6 October 2026</p>

      <h2>1. Who we are</h2>
      <p>
        Pelerin is made by Luvita. Contact:
        <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. For the account
        data in section 5, Luvita is the data controller (KVKK, Article 3).
      </p>

      <h2>2. What the app does</h2>
      <p>
        Pelerin runs on your own computer. You paste text into it. It finds
        personal identifiers in that text (for example names, ID numbers, IBANs
        and phone numbers) and replaces them with placeholders. You can copy the
        masked text into another tool yourself. If you paste an answer back, the
        app puts the original values back in, on your screen. To use it you sign
        in with an account that Luvita created for your office.
      </p>

      <h2>3. What we receive, and what we never receive</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>What Pelerin sends to Luvita:</strong> your account identity,
        your plan and counts of how many maskings you made by type and outcome.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What it never sends:</strong> your text, the masked text, the
        mapping between placeholders and original values, or your launch
        credential.
      </p>
      <p>
        In detail, the only connection Pelerin makes to Luvita is a licence
        check, made over HTTPS by the app's shell (not by the window you type
        in):
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>When you sign in, once:</strong> your office code, your login
          and your password, and a device label made of the name of your
          operating system and four random characters (never the name of your
          computer). The password is not kept by the app; we keep it only as a
          one-way hash.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>At launch, about once a day while the app runs, and after you
          sign in or out:</strong> a device credential the app received when you
          signed in, the app's version number, and counts. A count says, for
          example, that 12 phone numbers and 3 IBANs were masked and 2 values
          restored. It never says which values, or from which text.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What comes back:</strong> a signed licence that says which plan
          you are on, and your daily limit if the plan has one.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The app makes no other outside connection of its own. It makes no
        connection to any AI service.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Where your text goes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The app's own engine, on your computer.</strong> The window you
          see is served by an engine that runs on the same computer and listens
          only on that computer's loopback address. Nothing outside your computer
          can reach it. A new access code is created for every launch and is held
          in memory only.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not to Luvita and not to any AI service.</strong> Your text, the
          masked text and the placeholders never leave your computer through
          Pelerin. Anything you do with the masked text afterwards, such as
          pasting it into an AI tool, is your own act.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The original values live in memory only.</strong> The table that
          links each placeholder to its original value is held in the engine's
          memory and is gone when you close the app.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>5. Your account and plan: what we keep, why, where and for how long</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What we hold.</strong> Your office code; your login; your
          password as a one-way hash (argon2id), never the password itself; the
          email address your administrator gave if you were invited by email; your
          role; your office's plan (a trial, the free daily limit, or a paid
          period); one record for each computer you signed in on (its label, when
          it was created, when it ends); the daily counts described in section 3;
          and a record of sign-in attempts (which account, success or failure,
          and the time) kept for up to 90 days.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Why.</strong> To let you sign in, to give your office the plan
          it has, to end access when someone leaves or a computer is lost, to keep
          the service secure and to understand how the pilot is used. The legal
          basis is that this is necessary to perform the licence contract with
          your office (KVKK Article 5(2)(c)).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Who sees it.</strong> Luvita, and the administrator of your
          office, who can list and end your office's computers. Luvita sees the
          counts, by day, type and outcome. We cannot see any text, because we
          never receive any.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Where.</strong> On servers in the European Union, outside
          Türkiye (our account service runs in Frankfurt, Germany). This means
          this account data is processed abroad. It does not include any text you
          handle with the app.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How long.</strong> A computer's record is kept while it is
          signed in and for 30 days after you sign out or it expires, then
          deleted. Sign-in attempts are kept for up to 90 days. Your account and
          the daily counts are kept while your office has an account with us; you
          can ask us to delete or correct your data at any time (section 10).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sign out.</strong> Signing out in the app ends that computer's
          access on our side and deletes the credential from your computer. Your
          administrator can also end it.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>6. What stays on your computer</h2>
      <p>
        The app writes a few files on your computer. They contain no text you
        pasted and no original values.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A record of placeholders</strong>
          (<code>Pelerin\audit\…\records.jsonl</code>): one line for each
          placeholder issued, with its placeholder code (for example a type name
          and a number), the time, and technical hashes. It shows how many
          identifiers of which types were found in a request. It never contains
          the text or the original values.
        </li>
        <li>
          A small usage counter file next to it. This is the file the counts in
          section 3 are read from.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your sign-in, in Windows Credential Manager:</strong> the
          device credential and the latest signed licence, under the app's own
          entry. They are kept in the operating system's protected credential store,
          not in the app's own data files. Signing out deletes them; if you uninstall without signing
          out they can stay behind, and your administrator can end the computer's
          access.
        </li>
        <li>
          Temporary working files of the engine, in a folder under
          <code>C:\ProgramData</code> whose name starts with <code>pd</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          The window's browser profile (cache and settings of the Microsoft Edge
          WebView2 component that draws the window), in
          <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\dev.cosmocrew.pelerin.desktop</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        When you uninstall the app from the Microsoft Store, the app's own folder
        is removed. The <code>C:\ProgramData\pd…</code> folder and the browser
        profile folder can stay behind. You can delete them yourself at any time.
        We do not read them.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Microsoft and Windows</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Microsoft Edge WebView2 Runtime.</strong> The app needs this
          Windows component to draw its window (Windows 11 and updated Windows 10
          already include it). It is a Microsoft product with its own behaviour
          and may connect to Microsoft services. We do not control that.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Microsoft Store.</strong> Microsoft collects installation and
          usage information about Store apps and gives us reports in aggregate,
          such as how many people installed the app and failure counts with
          technical stack traces. We use these reports only to keep the app
          working. Microsoft's own privacy statement governs what Microsoft
          collects.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Crash reports.</strong> If the app stops unexpectedly, Windows
          Error Reporting may keep or send a crash report to Microsoft, depending
          on your Windows diagnostic data settings. Such a report could include
          information that was in the app's memory at that moment. If you handle
          sensitive text, check your Windows diagnostic data settings.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>8. What you do with masked text</h2>
      <p>
        Pelerin keeps raw identifiers on your computer and materially reduces the
        risk of re-identification, disclosure and breach in the text you send
        onward. It does not mean that no personal data leaves your computer, and
        it does not remove your own obligations. If you send masked text to a
        service outside Türkiye, the legal basis for that transfer under Article
        9 of the Turkish Personal Data Protection Law (KVKK, Law No. 6698) is
        still yours to provide.
      </p>

      <h2>9. Security</h2>
      <p>
        The engine listens on your computer only, uses a per-launch access code
        held in memory, and keeps the original values in memory only. The licence
        check uses HTTPS, and the app trusts only licences signed by Luvita. It
        checks the signature with a public verification key built into the app;
        the signing key itself is never in the app. No system is perfectly
        secure, and the
        files listed in section 6 are protected only as well as your own Windows
        account and disk.
      </p>

      <h2>10. Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        For the account data in section 5, you have the rights listed in Article
        11 of KVKK, including to learn whether we process your data, to ask for it
        to be corrected or deleted, and to object. Write to
        <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>; we answer within
        the time the law sets. We do not hold, and cannot give you, text you
        handled with the app, because we never receive it. For data Microsoft
        holds about you, contact Microsoft. If you write to us, we use your
        message only to answer it; see our
        <a href={localizedPath('en', 'privacy')}>website privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>11. Children</h2>
      <p>Pelerin is not made for children.</p>

      <h2>12. Changes</h2>
      <p>
        We will publish changes to this policy at this address and change the
        date above. A change that affects what the app sends or does with your
        data will come with a new version of the app.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
